          "collection_date": "2011-09",
          "description": {
            "comment": "Paragonimus kellicotti is a trematode (Platyhelminthes) that causes human lung fluke disease (paragonimiasis). Adult worms live in the lungs of various mammals that feed on crayfish. People occasionally become infected by eating uncooked crayfish containing larval stages of the fluke. This species of fluke is widespread in North America east of the Rocky Mountains. Adult worms for genome sequencing were isolated from gerbils experimentally infected with larvae collected from Missouri crayfish.",
